UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 16: Personal finance and budgeting', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Recording spending means writing the date, item, and amount soon after you spend. Receipts and bank/app notes help you compare the plan with what actually happened. Without records, a budget is only a guess.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhich record is more useful for checking a $10 snack plan: a pile of unmarked coins, or a dated list of each snack purchase?\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe dated list lets you add amounts and compare with $10. Unmarked coins do not show what was spent when.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking memory alone is a record. Memory drops details; a written line survives the week.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a three-line spending record for a fictional day (date, item, amount). Total the amounts and compare with a $15 daily limit.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Commerce","learning_intention":"Recording spending means writing the date, item, and amount soon after you spend. Receipts and bank/app notes help you compare the plan with what actually happened. Without records, a budget is only a guess.","lesson_number":16,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CO5-CON-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking 